        "value": "Multiple politeness distinctions",
        "source": "wals-v2020.5",
        "note": "<div class=\"Example\"><table class=\"example\"><tr><td><p><i>ikaw</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg.</p></td><td><p>- the 2nd person sg pro is used, if the hearer is a relative ofthe younger generation, a friend, a social inferior person, amongchildren and adolescents age-mates, and to address God</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>kayo</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- the 2nd pers pl pronoun is politely used if there is a socialdistance between the interlocutors, i.e. if a child addresses anadult, an employee an employer, between adults which are sociallyequal, but not intimate, to relatives which belong to the oldergeneration (regardless actual age)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>sila</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hhon.</p></td><td><p>- the 3rd person pl pronoun is occasionally used to expressgreat respect and to mark great social distance; it is used toaddress monarchs or high politicians</p></td></tr></table></div>",
        "scale": "wals-v2020.5"
      }
    ]
